yzing row markers**\n\nI’m noticing that rows without any markers show zeroes, like row 7, which outputs a lot of vertical columns filled between markers. Initially, I thought all collinear points just needed three to connect. However, it seems like the interpolation method I used to fill in lines needs to account for horizontal and vertical entries. Row 7 has no markers, yet many vertical columns appear. So, I’m trying to understand how these markers in different columns affect the output.**Examining column fills**\n\nI see that in the demo, column 4 has marks, but there’s a curious point at row 15, column 3. I realize that I need to fill in column 4 when row 15 has a zero. Somehow, the output shows row 15, column 4 as 3, even without markers. I need to simplify how I fill in spans between markers. My original transformation didn't handle backgrounds well, and my test produced unexpected results. I wonder if the demo aligns with the original.","t":1790721416.576,"summary":true},{"type":"tool","name":"write","input":"{\n \"path\": \"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-openclawlearner-1790687857/attempts/d3ae97d7f78548fbbe6aa9202f4eeb9d/system/state/workspace/programs/task-3da4903b.py\",\n \"content\": \"def transform(grid):\\n    h, w = len(grid), len(grid[0])\\n    bg = max(set(v for row in grid for v in row), key=lambda v: sum(row.count(v) for row in grid))\\n    out = [row[:] for row in grid]\\n    for r in range(h):\\n        cs = [c for c, x in enumerate(grid[r]) if x != bg]\\n        for c in range(min(cs, default=0), max(cs, default=-1)+1):\\n            if grid[r][c] == bg and cs: out[r][c] = 3\\n    for c in range(w):\\n        rs = [r for r in range(h) if grid[r][c] != bg]\\n        for r in range(min(rs, default=0), max(rs, default=-1)+1):\\n            if grid[r][c] == bg and rs: out[r][c] = 3\\n    return out\\n\"\n}","t":1790721416.576,"stored":["programs/task-3da4903b.py"],"output":"Successfully wrote 607 bytes to /home/ubuntu/c